1
00:00:00,182 --> 00:00:03,682
Which choice best states
the main idea of the text?

2
00:00:04,504 --> 00:00:07,912
On these questions, most of
the wrong answers aren't false.

3
00:00:07,932 --> 00:00:08,948
They're true.

4
00:00:08,968 --> 00:00:11,040
They just aren't the main idea.

5
00:00:11,182 --> 00:00:13,653
That's the mistake this video fixes.

6
00:00:13,673 --> 00:00:15,724
You'll learn the two
kinds of these questions,

7
00:00:15,744 --> 00:00:19,010
a method for each, and
the five trap answers.

8
00:00:19,030 --> 00:00:21,868
Plus six original SAT-style questions,

9
00:00:21,888 --> 00:00:25,102
including a story, a
poem, and a harder one.

10
00:00:25,126 --> 00:00:29,606
Central Ideas and Details is one of
three skills in the Information and Ideas

11
00:00:29,626 --> 00:00:33,233
domain, which is about a quarter
of the Reading and Writing section.

12
00:00:33,375 --> 00:00:37,935
It tests one thing: did you understand
what the text actually says?

13
00:00:37,955 --> 00:00:39,455
It comes in two forms.

14
00:00:39,598 --> 00:00:45,006
The main idea question asks: which choice
best states the main idea of the text?

15
00:00:45,026 --> 00:00:48,255
The detail question asks
about one specific point,

16
00:00:48,275 --> 00:00:50,791
and usually starts with
according to the text,

17
00:00:50,811 --> 00:00:52,241
or based on the text.

18
00:00:52,320 --> 00:00:55,371
Passages come from science,
history and social studies,

19
00:00:55,391 --> 00:00:57,371
the humanities, and literature.

20
00:00:57,391 --> 00:01:00,620
And literature includes short
excerpts from stories and poems,

21
00:01:00,640 --> 00:01:02,355
which get these same questions.

22
00:01:02,380 --> 00:01:05,359
Don't mix these up with
inference questions.

23
00:01:05,379 --> 00:01:06,396
Those end with a blank.

24
00:01:06,416 --> 00:01:07,360
These have no blank.

25
00:01:07,380 --> 00:01:08,844
They ask what the text says.

26
00:01:08,928 --> 00:01:12,873
For a main idea question, sum up the
whole passage in one sentence before you

27
00:01:12,893 --> 00:01:14,051
look at the choices.

28
00:01:14,071 --> 00:01:16,943
Who or what is it about, and
what's the point about it?

29
00:01:16,963 --> 00:01:18,106
Watch for a turn.

30
00:01:18,356 --> 00:01:23,301
Words like but, yet, however, and in
fact often mark where the main point

31
00:01:23,321 --> 00:01:24,185
starts.

32
00:01:24,205 --> 00:01:25,864
Then match.

33
00:01:25,884 --> 00:01:28,399
The right answer covers the whole passage.

34
00:01:28,419 --> 00:01:32,348
Not one sentence of it, and not
more than the passage covers.

35
00:01:32,705 --> 00:01:34,042
Think of it as a title.

36
00:01:34,062 --> 00:01:35,919
It has to fit the whole thing.

37
00:01:35,939 --> 00:01:39,311
For a detail question, don't summarize.

38
00:01:39,331 --> 00:01:40,276
Hunt.

39
00:01:40,296 --> 00:01:43,132
Find the exact line that
answers the question.

40
00:01:43,152 --> 00:01:45,633
The right answer says the
same thing in different words.

41
00:01:45,653 --> 00:01:48,831
If you can't point to a
line, it's not the answer.

42
00:01:48,859 --> 00:01:50,017
Question one.

43
00:01:50,037 --> 00:01:53,232
Leafcutter ants, which live
in warm parts of the Americas,

44
00:01:53,252 --> 00:01:55,482
are often seen marching in long lines,

45
00:01:55,502 --> 00:01:57,767
each ant holding up a piece of leaf.

46
00:01:57,787 --> 00:01:59,965
But the ants don't eat the leaves.

47
00:02:00,067 --> 00:02:02,118
Back in the nest, they crush the pieces,

48
00:02:02,138 --> 00:02:07,618
cut them even smaller, and use them to
feed a fungus that grows in underground

49
00:02:07,638 --> 00:02:08,547
gardens.

50
00:02:08,567 --> 00:02:12,153
The fungus, in turn, feeds
the ants and their young.

51
00:02:12,173 --> 00:02:15,009
In other words, leafcutter
ants are farmers.

52
00:02:15,029 --> 00:02:16,315
Pause and try it.

53
00:02:19,574 --> 00:02:21,038
Summarize first.

54
00:02:21,288 --> 00:02:25,018
Notice the turn: but the
ants don't eat the leaves.

55
00:02:25,038 --> 00:02:26,375
That's where the point starts.

56
00:02:26,395 --> 00:02:28,804
They use the leaves to grow a fungus,

57
00:02:28,824 --> 00:02:30,696
and the fungus is the food.

58
00:02:30,716 --> 00:02:35,109
One sentence: these ants use
leaves to farm their food.

59
00:02:35,150 --> 00:02:36,737
Choice D says exactly that.

60
00:02:36,757 --> 00:02:41,809
They gather leaves not to eat them, but
to grow a fungus that feeds the colony.

61
00:02:41,829 --> 00:02:42,954
That's the answer.

62
00:02:42,974 --> 00:02:47,546
Now B: they're often seen marching
in long lines carrying leaves.

63
00:02:47,760 --> 00:02:48,311
That's true.

64
00:02:48,331 --> 00:02:49,653
It's the first sentence.

65
00:02:49,760 --> 00:02:51,633
But it's the setup, not the point.

66
00:02:51,653 --> 00:02:55,596
A true detail that isn't the main
idea is the most common trap here.

67
00:02:55,616 --> 00:02:57,081
It's too narrow.

68
00:02:57,131 --> 00:03:00,917
C has the opposite problem:
many kinds of insects.

69
00:03:01,060 --> 00:03:03,560
The passage is about one kind of ant.

70
00:03:03,595 --> 00:03:04,861
That's too broad.

71
00:03:04,881 --> 00:03:07,967
And A, carrying fifty
times their own weight,

72
00:03:07,987 --> 00:03:11,273
may be true in real life,
but the text never says it.

73
00:03:11,296 --> 00:03:12,760
Now a detail question.

74
00:03:13,224 --> 00:03:15,474
In the summer of eighteen fifty-eight,

75
00:03:15,510 --> 00:03:19,882
hot weather made the sewage-filled River
Thames in London give off a powerful

76
00:03:19,902 --> 00:03:23,490
stench, an event that became
known as the Great Stink.

77
00:03:23,510 --> 00:03:27,633
At the time, most people believed that
diseases such as cholera were spread by

78
00:03:27,653 --> 00:03:29,242
foul-smelling air.

79
00:03:29,262 --> 00:03:32,170
With the smell drifting into their
own building beside the river,

80
00:03:32,190 --> 00:03:37,491
members of Parliament quickly passed
a law to fund a vast new sewer system.

81
00:03:37,511 --> 00:03:41,992
According to the text, what did most
people at the time believe about diseases

82
00:03:42,012 --> 00:03:43,226
such as cholera?

83
00:03:43,277 --> 00:03:44,562
Pause and try it.

84
00:03:47,821 --> 00:03:50,070
Detail question, so find the line.

85
00:03:50,107 --> 00:03:54,658
At the time, most people believed that
diseases such as cholera were spread by

86
00:03:54,678 --> 00:03:56,214
foul-smelling air.

87
00:03:56,284 --> 00:03:57,801
Choice B says the same thing.

88
00:03:57,821 --> 00:03:58,927
That's the answer.

89
00:03:58,959 --> 00:04:02,010
Now A: water contaminated with sewage.

90
00:04:02,030 --> 00:04:04,352
That's actually how cholera spreads.

91
00:04:04,602 --> 00:04:07,296
But the question asks
what people believed then,

92
00:04:07,316 --> 00:04:08,759
according to the text.

93
00:04:08,779 --> 00:04:10,510
A is outside knowledge.

94
00:04:10,530 --> 00:04:13,530
It isn't in the passage,
so it can't be the answer.

95
00:04:13,567 --> 00:04:17,833
C takes hot weather from the passage
and attaches it to the wrong thing.

96
00:04:17,853 --> 00:04:20,618
The heat caused the
smell, not the disease.

97
00:04:20,638 --> 00:04:22,903
And D adds the word only.

98
00:04:22,923 --> 00:04:24,816
Nothing in the text says that.

99
00:04:24,859 --> 00:04:26,161
Question three.

100
00:04:26,181 --> 00:04:30,982
In parts of Africa, a bird called the
greater honeyguide works with human honey

101
00:04:31,002 --> 00:04:32,002
hunters.

102
00:04:32,037 --> 00:04:34,446
The bird leads people to wild bees' nests,

103
00:04:34,466 --> 00:04:37,323
which are often hidden high up in trees.

104
00:04:37,430 --> 00:04:39,661
The hunters calm the bees with smoke,

105
00:04:39,681 --> 00:04:41,920
open the nest, and harvest the honey.

106
00:04:41,940 --> 00:04:46,011
The honeyguide, which would have trouble
breaking into such a nest by itself,

107
00:04:46,118 --> 00:04:50,618
then feeds on the wax combs left behind.

108
00:04:51,010 --> 00:04:54,455
According to the text, what does the
honeyguide gain from working with honey

109
00:04:54,475 --> 00:04:55,266
hunters?

110
00:04:55,286 --> 00:04:56,572
Pause and try it.

111
00:04:59,831 --> 00:05:01,810
Find the line about what the bird gets.

112
00:05:01,830 --> 00:05:03,203
It's the last sentence.

113
00:05:03,223 --> 00:05:06,061
The bird would have trouble
breaking into the nest by itself,

114
00:05:06,081 --> 00:05:08,525
and it feeds on the wax combs left behind.

115
00:05:08,545 --> 00:05:12,346
So it gets wax from nests
it couldn't open alone.

116
00:05:12,366 --> 00:05:13,687
That's choice C.

117
00:05:13,739 --> 00:05:15,524
Now watch who does what.

118
00:05:15,631 --> 00:05:18,203
A gives the bird a share of the honey,

119
00:05:18,239 --> 00:05:20,131
but the hunters take the honey.

120
00:05:20,239 --> 00:05:22,203
The bird gets the wax.

121
00:05:22,345 --> 00:05:25,076
B says the bird gets help finding nests,

122
00:05:25,096 --> 00:05:27,595
but the bird is the
one leading the people.

123
00:05:27,618 --> 00:05:30,954
Both choices use the right words,
attached to the wrong person.

124
00:05:30,974 --> 00:05:32,419
That's the wrong person trap.

125
00:05:32,439 --> 00:05:36,383
And D, a safe place to build
a nest, isn't in the text.

126
00:05:36,403 --> 00:05:38,127
It just borrows a phrase.

127
00:05:38,147 --> 00:05:39,341
Now literature.

128
00:05:39,361 --> 00:05:42,556
Story and poem passages
get the same questions,

129
00:05:42,576 --> 00:05:44,270
but your summary changes.

130
00:05:44,290 --> 00:05:47,520
Ask: who's in the scene,
what do they feel or want,

131
00:05:47,540 --> 00:05:48,754
and what changes?

132
00:05:49,075 --> 00:05:52,397
Most literary passages
are built around a shift.

133
00:05:52,445 --> 00:05:53,445
Two rules.

134
00:05:53,516 --> 00:05:55,388
First, read the line at the top.

135
00:05:55,408 --> 00:05:57,425
It tells you where the text comes from,

136
00:05:57,445 --> 00:05:59,588
and sometimes who the characters are.

137
00:05:59,873 --> 00:06:02,730
Second, don't invent a backstory.

138
00:06:02,765 --> 00:06:06,290
If the text doesn't say it,
the answer can't depend on it.

139
00:06:06,310 --> 00:06:07,361
Question four.

140
00:06:07,381 --> 00:06:11,131
The following text is from a short
story written for this practice set.

141
00:06:11,595 --> 00:06:16,021
Ana has recently taken over her
grandmother's flower stall at a city market.

142
00:06:16,041 --> 00:06:20,183
Ana had watched her grandmother
wrap bouquets a thousand times,

143
00:06:20,255 --> 00:06:25,076
but her first attempt fell apart, the
stems sliding loose from the paper.

144
00:06:25,433 --> 00:06:28,306
The customer, an older man in a gray coat,

145
00:06:28,326 --> 00:06:29,791
waited silently.

146
00:06:30,183 --> 00:06:31,933
Ana's face grew hot.

147
00:06:31,955 --> 00:06:36,221
On her second try, she slowed down and
folded the paper the way she remembered,

148
00:06:36,241 --> 00:06:39,955
corner over corner, and this time it held.

149
00:06:40,098 --> 00:06:44,577
The man nodded once, paid, and left
with the flowers tucked under his arm.

150
00:06:44,597 --> 00:06:47,955
Which choice best states
the main idea of the text?

151
00:06:47,982 --> 00:06:49,268
Pause and try it.

152
00:06:53,526 --> 00:06:54,919
Summarize the scene.

153
00:06:55,098 --> 00:06:56,526
Who: Ana.

154
00:06:56,812 --> 00:07:00,292
What changes: her first try falls apart,

155
00:07:00,312 --> 00:07:01,148
and she's embarrassed.

156
00:07:01,168 --> 00:07:04,899
Then she slows down, does it the
way she remembers from watching her

157
00:07:04,919 --> 00:07:06,613
grandmother, and it works.

158
00:07:06,633 --> 00:07:07,916
That's choice A.

159
00:07:07,936 --> 00:07:08,523
B is true.

160
00:07:08,543 --> 00:07:11,220
She had watched her
grandmother many times.

161
00:07:11,757 --> 00:07:13,916
But that's background from the first line,

162
00:07:13,936 --> 00:07:15,399
not the main idea.

163
00:07:15,649 --> 00:07:20,043
C says she realizes she'll never wrap
flowers as well as her grandmother.

164
00:07:20,150 --> 00:07:21,130
Never?

165
00:07:21,150 --> 00:07:23,328
The text shows her succeeding.

166
00:07:23,361 --> 00:07:27,647
Words like never and always need
a passage that's just as strong.

167
00:07:27,826 --> 00:07:31,683
And D makes the customer an old
friend of her grandmother's.

168
00:07:31,861 --> 00:07:34,576
That might be a good story,
but it isn't this one.

169
00:07:34,611 --> 00:07:37,575
An invented backstory is not in the text.

170
00:07:37,601 --> 00:07:38,510
Question five.

171
00:07:38,530 --> 00:07:41,637
The following text is from a poem
written for this practice set.

172
00:07:41,923 --> 00:07:43,831
My father called the river lazy.

173
00:07:43,851 --> 00:07:46,367
All August it dawdled past our fence,

174
00:07:46,387 --> 00:07:48,652
too tired, he said, to carry a leaf.

175
00:07:48,672 --> 00:07:50,540
I believed him for years.

176
00:07:50,560 --> 00:07:54,254
Then came the April it
rose and took the fence,

177
00:07:54,274 --> 00:07:59,290
the shed, the road he drove to work, and
I understood at last what it had been

178
00:07:59,310 --> 00:08:00,254
saving.

179
00:08:00,274 --> 00:08:05,279
Based on the text, how does the
speaker's view of the river change?

180
00:08:05,299 --> 00:08:06,585
Pause and try it.

181
00:08:10,843 --> 00:08:12,022
Track the shift.

182
00:08:12,415 --> 00:08:15,002
Before: the father calls the river lazy,

183
00:08:15,022 --> 00:08:16,629
and the speaker believes him.

184
00:08:16,736 --> 00:08:18,271
Then the flood.

185
00:08:18,557 --> 00:08:22,966
After: the speaker understands
what the river had been saving,

186
00:08:22,986 --> 00:08:24,058
its power.

187
00:08:24,093 --> 00:08:28,002
From accepting the father's view to
seeing the force behind the calm.

188
00:08:28,022 --> 00:08:29,362
That's choice B.

189
00:08:29,382 --> 00:08:30,382
Now D.

190
00:08:30,596 --> 00:08:34,540
The father calls the river lazy, then
later warns the speaker that it's

191
00:08:34,560 --> 00:08:35,560
dangerous.

192
00:08:35,810 --> 00:08:37,345
The first half is right.

193
00:08:37,596 --> 00:08:40,096
But the father never warns anyone.

194
00:08:40,381 --> 00:08:42,596
The realization belongs to the speaker.

195
00:08:42,845 --> 00:08:45,275
Same ideas, wrong person.

196
00:08:45,300 --> 00:08:46,780
A has it backward.

197
00:08:46,800 --> 00:08:51,479
The speaker never fears the river and
doesn't end up enjoying its slowness.

198
00:08:51,658 --> 00:08:55,479
And C, the family moving
away, isn't in the poem.

199
00:08:55,533 --> 00:08:57,655
So here are the five traps.

200
00:08:57,675 --> 00:09:00,763
Too narrow: a true detail
that isn't the point.

201
00:09:00,783 --> 00:09:03,083
Too broad: bigger than the passage.

202
00:09:03,103 --> 00:09:07,370
Not in the text: outside knowledge,
or an invented backstory.

203
00:09:07,390 --> 00:09:10,747
Too extreme: never, always, proves.

204
00:09:10,926 --> 00:09:15,411
And wrong person: the right idea,
attached to the wrong person.

205
00:09:15,431 --> 00:09:18,767
On harder questions, a wrong
choice can be half right.

206
00:09:18,787 --> 00:09:21,839
It starts out correct, then
slips in one of these traps.

207
00:09:21,859 --> 00:09:22,714
Here's one.

208
00:09:22,734 --> 00:09:24,020
Question six.

209
00:09:24,127 --> 00:09:29,643
A popular story holds that scholars
opposed Christopher Columbus's plan to sail

210
00:09:29,663 --> 00:09:32,876
west to Asia because they
believed Earth was flat.

211
00:09:33,091 --> 00:09:36,071
Historians have found little
evidence for this story.

212
00:09:36,091 --> 00:09:41,448
Educated Europeans of the fourteen nineties
widely accepted that Earth is round.

213
00:09:41,475 --> 00:09:44,296
The real dispute was over its size.

214
00:09:44,330 --> 00:09:46,632
Columbus's critics argued, correctly,

215
00:09:46,652 --> 00:09:50,045
that he had badly underestimated
the distance to Asia.

216
00:09:50,188 --> 00:09:53,452
The flat Earth version became
popular centuries later,

217
00:09:53,472 --> 00:09:57,310
in part through an eighteen twenty-eight
biography of Columbus by Washington

218
00:09:57,330 --> 00:09:58,310
Irving.

219
00:09:58,330 --> 00:10:01,759
Which choice best states
the main idea of the text?

220
00:10:01,782 --> 00:10:02,726
Pause and try it.

221
00:10:02,746 --> 00:10:04,246
Take a little longer on this one.

222
00:10:08,453 --> 00:10:09,254
Summarize.

223
00:10:09,274 --> 00:10:11,381
It opens with a popular story.

224
00:10:11,452 --> 00:10:15,504
Then the turn: historians have
found little evidence for it.

225
00:10:15,524 --> 00:10:18,790
Everything after that
explains what really happened,

226
00:10:18,810 --> 00:10:20,575
and where the myth came from.

227
00:10:20,595 --> 00:10:23,647
One sentence: the flat
Earth story is a myth,

228
00:10:23,667 --> 00:10:26,468
and the real argument was about size.

229
00:10:26,488 --> 00:10:27,774
That's choice C.

230
00:10:27,824 --> 00:10:32,590
B is the toughest trap: the critics
correctly argued that he had underestimated

231
00:10:32,610 --> 00:10:33,590
the distance.

232
00:10:33,610 --> 00:10:35,804
That's true, and it's an important line.

233
00:10:35,824 --> 00:10:39,876
But it says nothing about the popular
story the whole passage is built around.

234
00:10:39,896 --> 00:10:41,503
True, but too narrow.

235
00:10:41,572 --> 00:10:42,588
D is half right.

236
00:10:42,608 --> 00:10:44,944
Yes, the critics knew Earth was round.

237
00:10:44,964 --> 00:10:47,338
But D says they
underestimated the distance.

238
00:10:47,358 --> 00:10:48,945
It was Columbus who did that.

239
00:10:48,965 --> 00:10:52,337
That's the wrong person trap, hiding
in a choice that starts out correct.

240
00:10:52,357 --> 00:10:54,108
Read every word.

241
00:10:54,154 --> 00:10:57,777
And A, most stories
about famous explorers,

242
00:10:57,797 --> 00:11:00,562
is too broad and too extreme at once.

243
00:11:00,582 --> 00:11:03,005
The passage is about one story.

244
00:11:03,025 --> 00:11:04,326
Here's your test-day routine.

245
00:11:04,346 --> 00:11:08,203
Read the question first, so
you know which kind it is:

246
00:11:08,489 --> 00:11:10,631
main idea, or detail.

247
00:11:10,674 --> 00:11:14,082
Main idea: read the whole
passage, find the turn,

248
00:11:14,102 --> 00:11:17,333
and say the point in one sentence
before you look at the choices.

249
00:11:17,353 --> 00:11:20,082
Then pick the choice that
fits the whole passage,

250
00:11:20,102 --> 00:11:21,960
no more and no less.

251
00:11:21,999 --> 00:11:25,177
Detail: find the line
that answers the question.

252
00:11:25,214 --> 00:11:27,551
The right answer restates that line.

253
00:11:27,571 --> 00:11:31,622
Check who said or did what, and
cross out anything too extreme,

254
00:11:31,642 --> 00:11:33,821
too broad, or not in the text.

255
00:11:33,850 --> 00:11:37,473
You have a little over a
minute per question on average.

256
00:11:37,493 --> 00:11:40,259
Detail questions go fast
once you find the line,

257
00:11:40,279 --> 00:11:43,385
so save that time for the
harder main idea ones.

258
00:11:43,416 --> 00:11:44,416
Recap.

259
00:11:44,452 --> 00:11:49,825
Main idea: summarize first, then pick
the choice that fits the whole passage.

260
00:11:49,845 --> 00:11:52,345
Details: find the line that proves it.

261
00:11:52,487 --> 00:11:55,396
And remember: true isn't enough.

262
00:11:55,416 --> 00:11:57,267
It has to be the point.

263
00:11:57,287 --> 00:12:00,302
Every question here, including
the story and the poem,

264
00:12:00,322 --> 00:12:04,394
was written for this video,
in the style of the real test.

265
00:12:04,536 --> 00:12:08,517
For official practice, the College
Board's free Question Bank lets you filter

266
00:12:08,537 --> 00:12:10,644
for Central Ideas and Details.

267
00:12:10,676 --> 00:12:12,212
If this helped, subscribe.

268
00:12:12,354 --> 00:12:18,175
Next up: a new set of hard questions
to try before the March sixth SAT.
